    "field": "Conservation paleobiology",
    "problem": "Uncertainty persists regarding the primary drivers of Late Quaternary megafaunal extinctions, specifically in disentangling the effects of climate change versus human impacts; this paper tests if climate-driven habitat contraction alone was sufficient to cause the extinction of two South American giant ground sloth species.",
    "method": "The authors use Bioclimatic Envelope Modeling, integrating fossil records with paleoclimatic simulations for the Last Glacial Maximum and mid-Holocene, to model the potential distributions of two extinct giant sloth species and evaluate the impact of climate change on their extinction risk.",
    "task": "The task is to analyze how climatic changes during the last ice age affected the potential distributions and extinction risk of two extinct giant ground sloth species in South America using bioclimatic envelope modeling, fossil records, and paleoclimatic simulations.",
